From: Florian Fuchs <fuchsfl@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 19:00:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] sh: maple: Don't process the sent queue before its DMA ran

maple_dma_handler() treats an idle DMA engine as "the sent queue was
transferred". But the queue is armed for the VBLANK trigger, so the
engine also reads idle while the queue still waits for its transfer.
When a DMA end interrupt arrives while the handler is still running,
the work is queued again. The rerun then picks up the queue that the
first run just built with maple_send() and processes it before the
hardware sent it. The receive buffers still hold the responses of the
previous command of each device, which then get delivered as the answer
to the new one.

For input devices that is mostly harmless, but a VMU block read gets
the previous block, or the RESPONSE_OK of the previous block write,
which vmu-flash hands out as data. In QEMU this happened thousands of
times per boot and made vmufat lose directory entries, or loop on a
garbage FAT until the box ran out of memory.

Count the DMA end interrupts and remember the count when maple_send()
builds the sent queue. Leave the queue armed in maple_dma_handler()
until an interrupt arrived after it was built.

---
diff --git a/drivers/sh/maple/maple.c b/drivers/sh/maple/maple.c
--- a/drivers/sh/maple/maple.c
+++ b/drivers/sh/maple/maple.c
@@ -35,6 +35,9 @@
 static void maple_vblank_handler(struct work_struct *work);
 
 static DECLARE_WORK(maple_dma_process, maple_dma_handler);
+/* DMA end interrupts seen, and their count when the sent queue was built */
+static atomic_t maple_dma_irqs = ATOMIC_INIT(0);
+static int maple_dma_armed;
 static DECLARE_WORK(maple_vblank_process, maple_vblank_handler);
 
 static LIST_HEAD(maple_waitq);
@@ -308,6 +311,7 @@
 	}
 	mutex_unlock(&maple_wlist_lock);
 	if (maple_packets > 0) {
+		maple_dma_armed = atomic_read(&maple_dma_irqs);
 		for (i = 0; i < (1 << MAPLE_DMA_PAGES); i++)
 			__flush_purge_region(maple_sendbuf + i * PAGE_SIZE,
 					PAGE_SIZE);
@@ -644,6 +648,16 @@
 
 	if (!maple_dma_done())
 		return;
+	/*
+	* An idle DMA engine doesn't mean the sent queue was transferred: it
+	* may still wait for its VBLANK trigger, when this work was queued
+	* again by the interrupt of the previous transfer. Its receive
+	* buffers then still hold the previous responses, so leave it armed
+	* until the next DMA end interrupt.
+	*/
+	if (!list_empty(&maple_sentq) &&
+		atomic_read(&maple_dma_irqs) == maple_dma_armed)
+		return;
 	__raw_writel(0, MAPLE_ENABLE);
 	if (!list_empty(&maple_sentq)) {
 		list_for_each_entry_safe(mq, nmq, &maple_sentq, list) {
@@ -721,6 +735,7 @@
 static irqreturn_t maple_dma_interrupt(int irq, void *dev_id)
 {
 	/* Load everything into the bottom half */
+	atomic_inc(&maple_dma_irqs);
 	schedule_work(&maple_dma_process);
 	return IRQ_HANDLED;
 }
--
